+madagascar-split | 88 mya | India | nature, movement | all | Insular India | India breaks from Madagascar | India's separation from Madagascar began 88 million years ago, after the breakup of Gondwana. For the latest Cretaceous and most of the Paleocene it was an isolated landmass, known as Insular India. | About 88 million years ago India began to break away from Madagascar. For a long time after that it was a giant island.
+deccan-traps | 66.3 mya..65.6 mya | Deccan Traps | nature | deccan | Deccan Traps | The Deccan Traps erupt | The Deccan Traps of west-central India are one of the largest volcanic features on Earth. Their eruptions lasted 600,000 to 800,000 years, between about 66.3 and 65.6 million years ago, and left basalt more than 2 kilometres thick over about 500,000 square kilometres. | About 66 million years ago lava poured out over western India for hundreds of thousands of years. It left a layer of black rock two kilometres thick.
+collision-with-asia | 55 mya | Himalayas | nature | himalaya | Indian plate; Geology of the Himalayas | India meets Asia | The Indian plate moved north at about 20 cm a year and is believed to have begun colliding with Asia as early as 55 million years ago. The Himalayas are the result of that collision, which is still going on. | About 55 million years ago India began to push into Asia. The ground crumpled up and became the Himalaya.
+rajasaurus | 2003 | Narmada River | nature, science | west | Rajasaurus | Rajasaurus is named | Rajasaurus was a meat-eating dinosaur of Late Cretaceous India. Its bones were excavated from the Lameta Formation in Gujarat, and it was described in 2003 from a partial skeleton: a first for an Indian theropod. | In 2003 scientists named a meat-eating dinosaur from India: Rajasaurus. It lived where the Narmada River valley is today.
+vasuki | 2005 | Kutch district | nature, science | west | Vasuki indicus | A giant fossil snake | Vasuki indicus is an extinct snake that lived in India during the Middle Eocene. Its estimated length of 10.9 to 15.2 metres makes it the largest of its family. The fossils were discovered in 2005 in a lignite mine. | In 2005 the bones of a giant snake were found in a mine in Gujarat. It was between 11 and 15 metres long and lived tens of millions of years ago.
+indohyus | 1970 | Kashmir Valley | nature, science | himalaya | Indohyus | A relative of the whales | Indohyus is an extinct animal known from Eocene fossils found in rocks collected in Kashmir in 1970. About the size of a fox, it shared some traits with whales and showed signs of adaptation to life in water. | Fossils found in rocks from Kashmir belong to a fox-sized animal that liked the water. It was a distant relative of the whales.
+lonar | 52 kya | Lonar Lake | nature, science | deccan | Lonar Lake | The Lonar crater | Lonar Lake in Maharashtra is a salty soda lake in a crater made by a meteorite impact. Earlier analyses dated it to about 52,000 years ago. A later method gave a much older age of 570,000 years, which is disputed. | Lonar Lake fills a hole made when a rock from space hit the Earth. Scientists do not agree on when: perhaps 52,000 years ago, perhaps much earlier.
+baba-budan | 1670 | Baba Budangiri | food, trade, religion | south | Baba Budan | Seven coffee beans | Baba Budan, a 17th-century Sufi whose shrine is at Baba Budangiri in Karnataka, is known to have first introduced the coffee plant to India. He brought seven raw beans from the port of Mokha in Yemen on his way back from the hajj in 1670. | In 1670 a holy man named Baba Budan came home from Arabia with seven coffee beans and planted them in the hills of Karnataka. That is how coffee came to India.
+assam-tea | 1823 | Assam | food, trade, work | northeast | Assam tea | Assam's tea bush is noticed | Assam tea is a black tea made from a variety of the tea plant native to Assam. Its introduction to Europe is linked to Robert Bruce, a Scottish adventurer who apparently encountered the plant in 1823. | In 1823 a Scottish traveller, Robert Bruce, came across the tea plant of Assam.
+chili | 15th century | India | food, trade | all | Chili pepper | The chilli crosses the ocean | European explorers carried chili peppers to the Old World in the late 15th century as part of the Columbian exchange, and many varieties came to be grown around the world. | Chillies first came to Europe, Africa and Asia on the ships of European explorers about 500 years ago.
+biryani | 17th century | India | food, daily | all | Biryani | Biryani in Mughal India | Biryani is a mixed rice dish traditionally made with spiced meat, seafood or vegetables. It was present in Mughal-era India, though whether it was created there is debated. | Biryani, a dish of rice cooked with spices and meat or vegetables, was eaten in the India of the Mughal emperors. Nobody is sure where it was invented.
+langar | 16th century | Golden Temple | food, religion, daily | northwest | Langar (Sikhism) | The langar, a kitchen for everyone | In Sikhism a langar is the community kitchen of a gurdwara, which serves meals free of charge to all, regardless of religion, caste, gender, wealth or ethnicity. It was started by the Sikh Gurus. | Every Sikh gurdwara has a kitchen called a langar where anyone at all can eat for free. The Sikh Gurus began the custom.
+dabbawalas | now | Mumbai | food, work, daily | west | Dabbawala | Mumbai's lunchbox carriers | A dabbawala is a worker who delivers hot lunches from homes and restaurants to people at work, especially in Mumbai. The dabbawalas form a lunchbox delivery and return system. | In Mumbai, workers called dabbawalas carry hot home-cooked lunches to people at their offices every day.
