+kala-azar | 1922 | India | medicine, science | east | Upendranath Brahmachari | A cure for kala-azar | In 1922 the physician and scientist Upendranath Brahmachari synthesised urea-stibamine and demonstrated that it was effective in treating kala-azar. | In 1922 Dr Upendranath Brahmachari made a medicine that cured a deadly disease called kala-azar.
+bhore-committee | 1943 | New Delhi | medicine, power | all | Bhore Committee | The Bhore Committee | The Bhore Committee, also known as the Health Survey and Development Committee, was set up by the Government of India in 1943 to survey the country's health. | In 1943 a committee was set up to study the health of India's people and plan how to improve it.
+ort | 1966 | Kolkata | medicine, science | east | Dilip Mahalanabis; Oral rehydration therapy | Sugar, salt and water | Oral rehydration therapy, drinking water with modest amounts of sugar and salts, prevents and treats dehydration from diarrhoea. The paediatrician Dilip Mahalanabis began researching it in Calcutta in 1966 and pioneered its use. | A simple drink of water, sugar and salt can save the life of someone with bad diarrhoea. Dr Dilip Mahalanabis began studying it in Calcutta in 1966.
+last-smallpox-major | 1975 | Bhola Island | medicine | bangladesh | Rahima Banu | The last case of deadly smallpox | Rahima Banu, born in 1972, is the last known person to have been infected with naturally occurring Variola major, the more deadly variety of smallpox. She caught it at three years old. | A little girl in Bangladesh named Rahima Banu, three years old, was the last person in the world to catch the deadliest kind of smallpox. She got better.
+midday-meal | now | New Delhi | food, science, daily | all | Midday Meal Scheme | Free lunch at school | The Midday Meal Scheme is a free school meal programme designed to improve the nutrition of school-age children. Serving 120 million children in over 1.27 million schools, it is the largest of its kind in the world. | In India 120 million schoolchildren get a free cooked lunch at school every day. It is the biggest school meal programme in the world.
+covaxin | 2021-11-03 | India | medicine, science | all | Covaxin | An Indian COVID-19 vaccine | Covaxin is a COVID-19 vaccine developed by Bharat Biotech with the Indian Council of Medical Research. On 3 November 2021 the World Health Organization validated it for emergency use, the first Indian-developed COVID-19 vaccine to be approved. | Indian scientists made their own vaccine against COVID-19. The World Health Organization approved it in November 2021.
