+velu-nachiyar | 1780 | Sivaganga | women, war, power | south | Velu Nachiyar | Velu Nachiyar wins back her kingdom | Rani Velu Nachiyar was queen of the Sivaganga estate from about 1780 to 1790. In 1780 she waged war against the British and successfully reclaimed her kingdom. She was the first Indian queen to wage war on the East India Company. | In 1780 Queen Velu Nachiyar of Sivaganga went to war against the British company and won her kingdom back.
+kittur-chennamma | 1824 | Kittur | women, war, power | south | Kittur Chennamma | Kittur Chennamma resists | Chennamma, queen of the princely state of Kittur in today's Karnataka, adopted an heir in 1824 after the deaths of her husband and son. She led an armed resistance against the British East India Company. | In 1824 Queen Chennamma of Kittur adopted a boy to be the next ruler. The British company would not accept this, and she fought them.
+savitribai-school | 1848 | Pune | women, science | deccan | Savitribai Phule | A school for girls in Pune | In 1848 Savitribai Phule, with Jyotirao Phule and Sagunabai Kshirsagar, established a school for girls at Bhide Wada in Pune. She is widely regarded as the first Indian female teacher. | In 1848 Savitribai Phule and her husband opened a school for girls in Pune. She is remembered as India's first woman teacher.
